Subject: [PATCH] quest POI: allow a cross-zone quest turn-in

infra#2801. Bots stood around with finished quests they could never hand in,
and it was never a pathfinding failure - they never attempted to move at all.

`NewRpgDoQuestAction::DoCompletedQuest` asks `GetQuestPOIPosAndObjectiveIdx`
where to hand a quest in. In its `toComplete` branch that lookup discarded any
reward POI outside the bot's CURRENT ZONE, and any 1500+ yards away. When every
candidate is discarded the vector comes back empty, the function returns false,
and `DoCompletedQuest` calls `ChangeToIdle()` at NewRpgAction.cpp:580-581 -
BEFORE the `MoveFarTo` at :604 is ever reached.

Measured on the live realm:

    Grug                (-9328,  -804)  Elwynn Forest, zone 12
    quest 109 reward    (-10509,  1045) Sentinel Hill, Westfall, MapID 0
    distance2d          2194 yards

Both filters rejected it independently - the zone check because Westfall is not
Elwynn, the cap because 2194 >= 1500 - so relaxing only one would have changed
nothing. All three of that character's completed quests turn in outside the
zone (109 Westfall, 1097 and 1638 Stormwind).

WHY THIS APPLIES TO EVERY BOT AND NOT A PRIVILEGED FEW

An earlier revision gated this on `IsRandomBot` so only operator-created
characters were freed, on the reasoning that the filters protect
RandomPlayerbotMgr's placement of the random population into level-appropriate
zones. The operator's decision is that every bot should have the same freedom:
they are meant to level up as a living world, and a population that cannot hand
in a quest whose giver is one zone away does not level.

The filters were never load-bearing for correctness. Nothing downstream needs
the POI to be in-zone - `MoveFarTo` routes to any same-map destination through
`PathGenerator` and already recovers from a wedged path by teleporting after
five failed attempts (NewRpgBaseAction.cpp:97-113). They were a containment
heuristic, and containment is precisely what is being traded away on purpose.

THE COST, STATED PLAINLY: every bot holding an out-of-zone turn-in may now
compute a long path, where before it computed none. On a realm running several
hundred bots on a worldserver already measured at 273% CPU that is a real
increase in pathfinding work, not a theoretical one. It is bounded by the 5000
cap, by `IsWaitingForLastMove` throttling recomputation while a spline is
walking, and by the fact that only bots with a COMPLETE out-of-zone quest are
affected at any moment rather than the whole population at once. It should be
watched after this lands.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE

  * `qPoi.MapId != bot->GetMapId()` - kept. There is no navmesh across the
    ocean; a cross-continent turn-in needs a boat or a zeppelin, and a bot that
    accepted such a POI would fail far more confusingly than a clean refusal.

  * The INCOMPLETE branch further down, which has its own copy of the 1500 cap.
    Turn-ins are BOUNDED - walk there, hand in, done. Objective-hunting is
    open-ended, and widening it would scatter parties across the world chasing
    mobs, a failure already measured on this realm at 937 yards of spread with
    the healer 600 yards from the tank.

  * The cap is RAISED to 5000, not removed. 2194 is what was actually needed;
    unbounded, a level 5 in Elwynn could set off for Booty Bay.

Verified against the pins in UPSTREAM-PINS.env:
  module 8d9f6aa6bc6d45f9ae0ee0675b9b1f8aa6937312
This revision uses NO new upstream member at all - it only changes a constant
and deletes a condition, so there is nothing here the compiler has not already
been proving on every build.

diff --git a/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgBaseAction.cpp b/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgBaseAction.cpp
index d213d0b..6a51181 100644
--- a/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgBaseAction.cpp
+++ b/src/Ai/World/Rpg/Action/NewRpgBaseAction.cpp
@@ -859,7 +859,13 @@ bool NewRpgBaseAction::GetQuestPOIPosAndObjectiveIdx(uint32 questId, std::vector
                 dy += point.y * weights[i];
             }
 
-            if (bot->GetDistance2d(dx, dy) >= 1500.0f)
+            // infra#2801: 5000 rather than 1500. A quest turn-in is very often
+            // in the next zone over - Grug's was 2194 yards away in Westfall
+            // while he stood in Elwynn - and at 1500 every such POI was
+            // discarded before the bot ever tried to walk. Raised rather than
+            // removed so a level 5 in Elwynn still will not set off for Booty
+            // Bay.
+            if (bot->GetDistance2d(dx, dy) >= 5000.0f)
                 continue;
 
             float dz = std::max(bot->GetMap()->GetHeight(dx, dy, MAX_HEIGHT), bot->GetMap()->GetWaterLevel(dx, dy));
@@ -867,8 +873,17 @@ bool NewRpgBaseAction::GetQuestPOIPosAndObjectiveIdx(uint32 questId, std::vector
             if (dz == INVALID_HEIGHT || dz == VMAP_INVALID_HEIGHT_VALUE)
                 continue;
 
-            if (bot->GetZoneId() != bot->GetMap()->GetZoneId(bot->GetPhaseMask(), dx, dy, dz))
-                continue;
+            // THE SAME-ZONE FILTER IS GONE. It is what stranded every bot with
+            // an out-of-zone turn-in: the POI was discarded, poiInfo came back
+            // empty, and DoCompletedQuest idled at NewRpgAction.cpp:580-581
+            // WITHOUT EVER REACHING THE MoveFarTo AT :604. The bot did not
+            // fail to walk there, it never attempted to. Nothing downstream
+            // needs the POI to be in-zone: MoveFarTo routes to any same-map
+            // destination through PathGenerator and teleport-recovers from a
+            // wedged path after five failed attempts (:97-113).
+            //
+            // The MapId check above is deliberately kept - there is no navmesh
+            // across an ocean, and a cross-continent turn-in needs a boat.
 
             poiInfo.push_back({{dx, dy}, qPoi.ObjectiveIndex});
         }
